.TH KITTY 3
.SH NAME
kitty \- serial terminal graphics and input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -b '#κ' /dev

.B /dev/kbd
.B /dev/kittyctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I kitty
device uses a dedicated serial port to display the kernel screen
in a terminal supporting the Kitty graphics protocol.
It provides a software framebuffer for the ordinary
.IR draw (3)
device, including the bitmap console and software cursor.
Mouse reports are delivered through
.IR mouse (3).
The device is currently included in the
.B pc
and
.B pc64
kernels.
.PP
Set
.BI kittyuart= n
and
.B monitor=kitty
in the kernel configuration.
The numbered UART must not be the serial console or already open.
It is reserved until reboot and configured for 115200 baud,
eight data bits, one stop bit, no parity, and no flow control.
Ordinary reads and writes to its
.B eia
and
.B eiactl
files are refused.
No port is opened and no screen is changed without
.BR kittyuart .
.PP
The terminal and the UART must pass bytes unmodified, without
local echo, line processing, or injected console messages.
QEMU can connect the serial port directly to a terminal, socket,
or pseudoterminal.
The checkout's
.B boot/qemu
launcher selects a second serial port for graphics with
.BR -kitty ;
the first port's console output defaults to
.B .qemu.console.log
in the exported root.
For example, from a Kitty-compatible terminal with a built kernel
and userland:
.IP
.EX
boot/qemu -amd64 -kitty
.EE
.PP
.B -kitty
cannot be combined with
.B -vga
or
.BR -cpuwait .
ARM64 and RISC-V screen and transport adapters are not provided.
If the launcher is killed before it restores terminal modes,
run the host's terminal reset command.
Rebooting the guest ends QEMU; otherwise stop QEMU from another
host terminal.
.PP
At startup the driver enters the alternate screen, negotiates
graphics and keyboard support, and requests the terminal's
pixel and character-cell dimensions.
The framebuffer initially measures 800 by 600 pixels.
Geometry reports resize it, up to 1920 by 1200 pixels;
dimensions smaller than 128 pixels are ignored.
Cell dimensions are limited to 128 pixels.
Resizing is also polled for terminals without in-band notifications.
Draw clients receive the usual mouse resize notification.
Do not reconfigure this screen using
.IR vga (3).
.PP
Dirty, cell-aligned tiles are copied out of the framebuffer,
converted to RGB, and transmitted in bounded inline base64 chunks.
Encoding, serial writes, and acknowledgement waits occur in a
separate kernel process, never in a drawing or interrupt callback.
Only one tile awaits a terminal status response at a time.
Damage is coalesced while the terminal is slow.
After five seconds without an acknowledgement, transmission stops.
.B Kittyctl
reports the connection status, screen and cell dimensions,
and transmission and input counters.
Writing
.B redraw
restarts negotiation and requests a full repaint.
.PP
.B Kbd
is an exclusive-reader stream of complete, NUL-terminated keyboard
messages understood by
.IR kbdfs (8).
The boot script binds it before starting
.B kbdfs
so the usual
.B /dev/cons
and
.B /dev/kbd
interfaces work without a new user-space decoder.
Kitty keyboard press, repeat, release, modifier, and associated-text
reports are accepted, with legacy escape-sequence fallback.
SGR pixel mouse reports, cell-coordinate fallback,
focus changes, and bracketed paste are supported.
Paste is streamed rather than accumulated; control characters
other than tab and newline are discarded.
Input sequences and pending key state have fixed size limits.
Input exceeding the queue limit is dropped and counted.
